1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,302
Sam did something nobody on the team had done in weeks: he clicked Harlan's

2
00:00:04,302 --> 00:00:08,518
best-performing ad on his own phone, the way a stranger scrolling past it would.

3
00:00:08,518 --> 00:00:12,787
The ad read "it's hour eight and your feet finally aren't screaming." The page it

4
00:00:12,787 --> 00:00:17,003
opened on read "quality socks for everyone." Nothing about the click was broken.

5
00:00:17,003 --> 00:00:19,638
It just didn't feel like the same promise anymore.

6
00:00:19,638 --> 00:00:24,064
One more document before the workshop, and it's the one people skip because it isn't

7
00:00:24,064 --> 00:00:25,382
technically an ad at all.

8
00:00:25,382 --> 00:00:29,545
You can build a perfect product truth document, a sharp hook matrix, and twenty

9
00:00:29,545 --> 00:00:33,866
genuinely distinct angles, and still watch a real winner lose, because the page it

10
00:00:33,866 --> 00:00:36,238
lands on breaks the promise the ad just made.

11
00:00:36,238 --> 00:00:40,612
That gap has a name, and it's worth understanding before Section seven's test tells

12
00:00:40,612 --> 00:00:43,405
you an angle failed when the angle was actually fine.

13
00:00:43,405 --> 00:00:47,674
It's called congruence: whether the page keeps talking about the exact thing that

14
00:00:47,674 --> 00:00:48,675
made someone click.

15
00:00:48,675 --> 00:00:52,100
It's the part of the funnel that belongs to nobody in particular.

16
00:00:52,100 --> 00:00:56,527
The person writing the ad usually isn't the person who owns the landing page, so the

17
00:00:56,527 --> 00:01:00,954
seam between them is exactly where things quietly fall apart without anyone deciding

18
00:01:00,954 --> 00:01:01,586
they should.

19
00:01:02,136 --> 00:01:04,886
Here's the gap, made concrete with one real click.

20
00:01:04,886 --> 00:01:09,287
Someone stops scrolling because "hour eight" named something they actually feel.

21
00:01:09,287 --> 00:01:12,477
They click, expecting the page to keep talking about that.

22
00:01:12,477 --> 00:01:17,097
Instead they land on a homepage that's technically about the same product but speaks

23
00:01:17,097 --> 00:01:19,352
a completely different, generic language.

24
00:01:19,352 --> 00:01:23,973
That half-second of "wait, is this the right place" is where a winning angle quietly

25
00:01:23,973 --> 00:01:28,373
becomes a losing ad, and nothing about the creative itself was ever the problem.

26
00:01:28,373 --> 00:01:32,114
This is easy to underestimate because nothing about it looks broken.

27
00:01:32,114 --> 00:01:35,909
The page loads fine, the product's right there, the price is correct.

28
00:01:35,909 --> 00:01:40,144
The failure is entirely about continuity of feeling, and that kind of failure

29
00:01:40,144 --> 00:01:41,409
doesn't throw an error.

30
00:01:41,409 --> 00:01:45,920
It just quietly loses the sale and leaves a number in a report that looks like the

31
00:01:45,920 --> 00:01:46,525
ad's fault.

32
00:01:46,525 --> 00:01:50,870
That's exactly the report Sam almost acted on before he clicked the ad himself.

33
00:01:50,870 --> 00:01:53,290
The dashboard said the angle underperformed.

34
00:01:53,290 --> 00:01:57,911
It never said why, and "why" turned out to have nothing to do with the angle at all.

35
00:01:58,461 --> 00:02:02,787
Four things actually have to carry through, and none of them require a rebuild of

36
00:02:02,787 --> 00:02:03,589
the whole site.

37
00:02:03,589 --> 00:02:07,862
The headline should echo the ad's language closely enough that a visitor doesn't

38
00:02:07,862 --> 00:02:10,640
have to re-read to check they're in the right place.

39
00:02:10,640 --> 00:02:15,073
The hero image should continue the visual promise, not switch to something generic.

40
00:02:15,073 --> 00:02:19,453
The offer and price need to match exactly, because any gap there reads as bait and

41
00:02:19,453 --> 00:02:21,590
switch even when it's an honest mistake.

42
00:02:21,590 --> 00:02:25,970
And whatever proof point earned the click, the spec, the review, the number, needs

43
00:02:25,970 --> 00:02:28,961
to actually appear on the page, not just live in the ad.

44
00:02:29,511 --> 00:02:33,010
One honest limit, because this isn't always a five-minute fix.

45
00:02:33,010 --> 00:02:37,751
Sometimes the real problem is a landing page that belongs to someone else, built for

46
00:02:37,751 --> 00:02:42,265
a different purpose, that nobody has the time or authority to rebuild before the

47
00:02:42,265 --> 00:02:43,281
test needs to run.

48
00:02:43,281 --> 00:02:45,143
When that's true, say so plainly.

49
00:02:45,143 --> 00:02:49,827
Blaming the ad, or worse, killing a genuinely good angle because the page undersold

50
00:02:49,827 --> 00:02:54,568
it, is a worse outcome than admitting the fix is a page problem you can't solve this

51
00:02:54,568 --> 00:02:54,850
week.

52
00:02:54,850 --> 00:02:59,365
There's a smaller version of this fix that's almost always available even when a

53
00:02:59,365 --> 00:03:04,105
full rebuild isn't: a dedicated section, or even a single line, that speaks the ad's

54
00:03:04,105 --> 00:03:07,773
exact language, added above whatever generic page already exists.

55
00:03:07,773 --> 00:03:10,539
It's not elegant, and it's not the real solution.

56
00:03:10,539 --> 00:03:14,828
It's often enough to close the worst of the gap without waiting on a project

57
00:03:14,828 --> 00:03:16,126
nobody's scheduled yet.

58
00:03:16,126 --> 00:03:20,697
That's the version Sam actually shipped after his own phone test: one new line at

59
00:03:20,697 --> 00:03:25,211
the top of the homepage, echoing "hour eight" word for word, nothing else on the

60
00:03:25,211 --> 00:03:25,945
page touched.

61
00:03:25,945 --> 00:03:28,315
It cost him twenty minutes, not a rebuild.

62
00:03:28,865 --> 00:03:33,022
Here's why this lecture earns its place before the workshop rather than after it.

63
00:03:33,022 --> 00:03:37,281
When Section eight teaches you to read a result honestly, "winner, loser, or noise"

64
00:03:37,281 --> 00:03:40,155
assumes the ad and the page were telling the same story.

65
00:03:40,155 --> 00:03:44,003
If they weren't, a loss might be telling you about the page, not the angle.

66
00:03:44,003 --> 00:03:48,160
Before you retire an angle for good, it's worth asking one question first: did it

67
00:03:48,160 --> 00:03:52,214
actually get a fair, congruent landing experience, or did it lose to a mismatch

68
00:03:52,214 --> 00:03:53,189
nobody checked for?

69
00:03:53,189 --> 00:03:57,499
Add this check to whatever kill rule you build in Section seven, right alongside the

70
00:03:57,499 --> 00:03:58,321
spend threshold.

71
00:03:58,321 --> 00:04:02,374
It costs thirty seconds per angle before a test launches, and it's cheaper than

72
00:04:02,374 --> 00:04:06,480
every one of those thirty seconds spent re-litigating a false negative after the

73
00:04:06,480 --> 00:04:07,609
spend is already gone.

74
00:04:07,609 --> 00:04:09,507
Make it a habit rather than a memory.

75
00:04:09,507 --> 00:04:13,715
A checklist you have to remember to run gets skipped on the one week it would have

76
00:04:13,715 --> 00:04:17,307
mattered most, which is usually the busiest week, not the calmest one.

77
00:04:17,857 --> 00:04:21,922
One lecture left in this section, and it's the one where you actually build

78
00:04:21,922 --> 00:04:22,464
something.

79
00:04:22,464 --> 00:04:26,854
Everything from the product truth document to this congruence checklist gets used

80
00:04:26,854 --> 00:04:29,184
for real, on a product you choose yourself.

81
00:04:29,184 --> 00:04:32,219
Before that, do Sam's five-minute test on your own site.

82
00:04:32,219 --> 00:04:36,501
Click your top-performing ad from your own phone, the way a stranger would, and

83
00:04:36,501 --> 00:04:40,295
check whether the headline still finishes the sentence the ad started.

84
00:04:40,295 --> 00:04:42,679
Fix what doesn't match now, while it's free.

85
00:04:42,679 --> 00:04:46,852
That fix belongs in the same brief you assemble next lecture as artifact B01.

86
00:04:46,852 --> 00:04:49,996
If nothing feels clearly broken, don't assume you're done.

87
00:04:49,996 --> 00:04:54,115
Sam's own mismatch didn't look broken either, right up until he read the two

88
00:04:54,115 --> 00:04:56,337
sentences back to back on his own screen.
